\section*{Depth-five homogeneous lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 135; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that characteristic-zero homogeneous depth-five circuits for iterated $n$-by-$n$ matrix multiplication need at least $n^{\sqrt n/400}$ gates, while block expansion supplies an upper bound of the same exponent scale.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 135; 2026-09-25), a rank measure built from differentiation followed by multiplication bounds each homogeneous circuit product. First and second trace moments for iterated multiplication give a much larger rank; pairing-type estimates control the second moment and force the gate lower bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 135; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 135; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: characteristic-zero homogeneous depth-five circuits for iterated $n$-by-$n$ matrix multiplication need at least $n^{\sqrt n/400}$ gates, while block expansion supplies an upper bound of the same exponent scale.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DepthFive.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Problem335.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

